;;; A form as a guard's argument read nothing, in silence — M1e2 **W24**,
;;; register row `W24/form-in-guard` (`corpus/wrong_answers.toml`), banked
;;; by S1e2.2.24 (2026-11-05) from its step 1's probe
;;; `build/s1e2.2.24/shapes/g-nested-rule.ein` (the program unchanged), ruled
;;; by the user on 2026-11-05 (Q-M1e2.18 (i)(a)); restated by M1f S1f.5.7 on
;;; the user's ruling of 2026-11-07 (Q-M1e2.26), which supersedes it.
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; Four facts of `p`, one of them the nested fact `(f a)`, and two rules
;;; whose guard compares `?x` with the nested fact `(f a)`.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; `eq` and `neq` are relations, and a nested fact is a value, as it is in
;;; `(p (f a))` (Q-M1e2.26). So the program loads, `e` fires on `(p (f a))`
;;; alone and `n` on `a`, `b` and `1`: root holds `(hit-eq (f a))`,
;;; `(hit-neq a)`, `(hit-neq b)` and `(hit-neq 1)`, its minimal line root's.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered, and why it does not any more ─────────────
;;;
;;; Until S1e2.2.24 the program loaded, and each guard compared the form as a
;;; raw IR node, equal to no value: `(eq ?x (f a))` never held, even where
;;; `?x` is bound to the fact `(f a)`, and `(neq ?x (f a))` always did. Root
;;; derived four `hit-neq` facts and no `hit-eq`, exit 0, with no word of the
;;; form (`ein saturate --dump FILE` and `ein solve FILE` at `f8c10730`).
;;; From S1e2.2.24 to M1f S1f.5.7 the loader refused it (Q-M1e2.18 (i)(a)),
;;; every command exiting 1 (`ein test`: 2). Since S1f.5.7's T1f.5.7.3
;;; (`6841faff`, 2026-11-09) it loads and answers as above, which the
;;; register's row holds (`ein-cli/tests/wrong_answers.rs`).
;;;

(relation p Node :why "p holds of {?1}") (relation q Node :why "q holds of {?1}") (relation f Node :why "f holds of {?1}") (relation hit-eq Node :why "hit-eq holds of {?1}") (relation hit-neq Node :why "hit-neq holds of {?1}")
(p a) (p b) (p 1) (p (f a)) (q a)
(rule e () :match (and (p ?x) (eq ?x (f a))) :assert (hit-eq ?x) :why "p holds of {?x}, which equals (f a), so hit-eq holds of {?x}")
(rule n () :match (and (p ?x) (neq ?x (f a))) :assert (hit-neq ?x) :why "p holds of {?x}, which is not (f a), so hit-neq holds of {?x}")
